      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "The target is Louisiana's state row for reporting_period 202608 and original_updated O in dataset 5abea2e0-3f8e-4b49-a50d-d63d5fd9103c, frozen at first publication. The dictionary identifies ex parte renewals as metric 5a1 and total successful renewals as metric 5a. I interpret share as 100 \u00d7 5a1 / 5a. The supplied ledger does not name its denominator, and CMS publishes both shares of successful renewals and rates among all renewals due; this interpretation is therefore an unresolved contract assumption."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Exact count-derived Louisiana history could not be fetched: the official catalogue identifies the September release CSV, but the web tool could not retrieve its contents or filtered API rows. Six original-release CMS snapshot tables were fetched instead. Their ex parte and total-renewed percentages are rounded and both use renewals due as denominator. historicalContext contains approximate successful-renewal shares calculated by dividing those printed percentages, not exact registered-series prints. The exact-history requirement remains unmet; this is an access limitation, not fewer than six available official prints."
      },
      {
        "kind": "tool",
        "tool": "official.lookup",
        "call": "Fetch Louisiana original renewal-outcome tables in https://www.medicaid.gov/resources-for-states/downloads/eligib-oper-and-enrol-snap-jan2026.pdf and https://www.medicaid.gov/resources-for-states/downloads/eligib-oper-and-enrol-snap-feb2026.pdf.",
        "result": "January 2026: 90,934 beneficiaries due, 58% renewed, 43% renewed ex parte; approximate successful-renewal share = 100 \u00d7 43 / 58 = 74.1379%. February 2026: 143,411 due, 49% renewed, 34% ex parte; approximate share = 100 \u00d7 34 / 49 = 69.3878%."
      },
      {
        "kind": "tool",
        "tool": "official.lookup",
        "call": "Fetch Louisiana original renewal-outcome tables in https://www.medicaid.gov/resources-for-states/downloads/eligib-oper-and-enrol-snap-mar2026.pdf and https://www.medicaid.gov/resources-for-states/downloads/eligib-oper-and-enrol-snap-apr2026.pdf.",
        "result": "March 2026: 108,555 beneficiaries due, 59% renewed, 40% renewed ex parte; approximate successful-renewal share = 100 \u00d7 40 / 59 = 67.7966%. April 2026: 129,160 due, 59% renewed, 41% ex parte; approximate share = 100 \u00d7 41 / 59 = 69.4915%."
      },
      {
        "kind": "tool",
        "tool": "official.lookup",
        "call": "Fetch Louisiana original renewal-outcome tables in https://www.medicaid.gov/resources-for-states/downloads/eligib-oper-and-enrol-snap-may2026.pdf and https://www.medicaid.gov/resources-for-states/downloads/eligib-oper-and-enrol-snap-jun2026.pdf.",
        "result": "May 2026: 111,446 beneficiaries due, 59% renewed, 41% renewed ex parte; approximate successful-renewal share = 69.4915%. June 2026: 96,868 due, 61% renewed, 42% ex parte; approximate share = 100 \u00d7 42 / 61 = 68.8525%."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Outside-view base rate: the six January\u2013June original-release proxies average 69.8596%. The March\u2013June average is 68.9080%, close to June's 68.8525%. This reference class favors persistence near the recent level rather than extrapolating January's higher share. All historical anchors use original monthly outcomes; updated submissions are excluded."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Policy mechanism: Louisiana RS 46:440.21 directs withdrawal of ex parte-authorizing waivers by January 1, 2026, while preserving exceptions expressly required by federal law. CMS's November 26, 2024 bulletin requires states to attempt ex parte renewals for all beneficiaries. These sources do not establish a total ban. Because the reference class follows the statutory deadline, I make no additional central policy adjustment; assuming existing restrictions are reflected in the recent observations is an inference."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "The official CMS snapshot index confirms June 2026 was released September 25, 2026, but provides no future announcement confirming December 15, 2026 for August. I retain the registered resolutionDate 2026-12-15 without inferring it from cadence. Official-calendar verification remains unmet."
      },
      {
        "kind": "math",
        "text": "Prior/update/interval: Use a persistence prior with modest shrinkage toward the March\u2013June mean, based on six fetched January\u2013June rounded original-release tables. Level = 0.75 \u00d7 68.8525 + 0.25 \u00d7 68.9080 = 68.8664%; momentum, one-off, and additional policy adjustments are each zero. Successive proxy changes are -4.7502, -1.5911, +1.6949, 0, and -0.6391 percentage points. Their sample standard deviation gives sigma = 2.3869 percentage points, calculated as sqrt(sum((change - mean_change)^2)/4). The one-month normal 80% half-width is 1.28 \u00d7 sigma = 3.0553. For the two-month June-to-August horizon, use independent monthly innovations and an additional rounding-error standard deviation of 0.5746, derived by propagating independent uniform half-percentage-point errors through 100 \u00d7 42 / 61. Half-width = 1.28 \u00d7 sqrt(2 \u00d7 2.3869^2 + 0.5746^2) = 4.3830. Unrounded bounds are 64.4834\u201373.2493%; emitted point and bounds are 68.9%, 64.5%, and 73.3%. This nominal interval uses approximate history and does not cover a different resolver-denominator interpretation."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "The upside risk is improved electronic matching or a cohort especially amenable to automated renewal, pushing the successful-renewal share above 73.3%. The downside risk is additional restrictions, failed data matches, or faster completion of form-based renewals, pushing it below 64.5%. A substantial systems or classification change could land outside the interval. Resolving against all renewals due instead of successful renewals would also invalidate this forecast's denominator assumption."
      },
